        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"constant-adolphe-a2893f43bb83ea52\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":2,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.93,\"analysis\":\"The utterance primarily presents Adolphe's love as presently true, so it has strong truth-conditional force even though the narration strongly suggests that the avowal is false or self-deceptive. In immediately reversing an attempted separation and asking Ellénore to return to him, it also has moderate force as a renewal of their relationship, though it contains no explicit promise. His crying out in an emotional crisis indicates intense delivery but does not independently establish expressive/reflexive force.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The extent of performative force is uncertain: the avowal effectively renews the relationship, but it may principally be an expedient reassurance rather than a deliberate undertaking. Adolphe may retain affection despite lacking the passionate love he claims.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Adolphe's spoken avowal to Ellénore, represented as direct speech within his retrospective first-person narration.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Adolphe has just told Ellénore that he no longer feels passionate love, intending to free himself and induce her to accept the count's offer. After she collapses, he reverses himself and claims both that he loves her tenderly and that his prior denial was a benevolent deception. Ellénore believes the avowal and renews her sacrifice, while the narrator undercuts it by saying that she mistakes her own love for a mutual one. Thus the utterance operates as a likely deceptive or self-deceptive avowal and relational reassurance whose practical effect is to bind Adolphe more deeply.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"« je vous aime d'amour, de l'amour le plus tendre »\",\"supports\":\"The explicit present-tense avowal strongly supports truth-conditional force.\",\"confidence\":1.0},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Adolphe says « revenez à moi » immediately before the avowal, after having attempted to end their bond.\",\"supports\":\"The avowal participates in reversing the separation and renewing or invoking their relationship, providing moderate performative support.\",\"confidence\":0.88},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The speech is introduced by « m'écriai-je » after Ellénore has collapsed.\",\"supports\":\"This establishes urgent and emotional delivery, but without evidence that the words themselves are a reflex-like discharge; it therefore does not warrant E support.\",\"confidence\":0.92},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The narrator calls the words « démenties par tant de paroles précédentes » and says Ellénore « s'enivra de son amour, qu'elle prenait pour le nôtre ».\",\"supports\":\"The retrospective narration strongly questions the avowal's sincerity or truth while leaving its assertoric mode intact.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Ellénore makes Adolphe repeat the words, confirms her refusal of the count's offer, and Adolphe finds himself « plus engagé que jamais ».\",\"supports\":\"The utterance functions as reassurance and has relationship-binding consequences, supporting some performative force without proving an explicit promise.\",\"confidence\":0.94}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T/P/E adequately represents the core force: a likely insincere avowal with some relationship-renewing force and emotionally urgent, but not independently reflexive, delivery. Deception and narrative irony belong to contextual interpretation rather than requiring O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
